course: dictionary
lesson: 14141
title: A wrong idea
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A misconception is a wrong idea about something. Many people hold it because they misunderstood, or because it looks right at first. «It's a misconception that bats are blind.» Bats can see, and many of them also use sound to find their way in the dark.
explain: Teachers look for misconceptions because they get in the way of learning. If a pupil thinks «heavier things always fall faster», the next lesson will be hard to follow until that idea is fixed. Finding the wrong idea first is the key step.
explain: Ask yourself: is this idea true every time, or only sometimes? When a text says «a common misconception», it is telling you that many people believe something that is not right. Check the facts before you accept the idea.
fact: A misconception is a wrong idea or belief, often one that many people share.
fact: «It's a misconception that the Sun goes round the Earth.» In fact, the Earth goes round the Sun.
fact: «Heavier things fall faster» is a misconception: in air without much drag, a heavy ball and a light ball fall at nearly the same speed.
fact: «Multiplying always makes a number bigger» is a misconception: multiplying by 0.5 makes a number smaller.
fact: Teachers clear up a misconception by showing what really happens and explaining why the wrong idea does not work.
exercise: Mia says, «It's a misconception that heavier things fall faster.» What does misconception mean? | A very heavy object | *A wrong idea or belief | A kind of test question | A fact that is always true
exercise: Which sentence shows a common misconception about maths? | *«Multiplying always makes a number bigger» | «Half of ten is five» | «Adding two numbers makes a bigger total» | «Four times two is eight»
exercise: A teacher wants to «clear up a misconception». What does she do? | Makes the wrong idea sound more exciting | *Shows that the wrong idea is wrong and explains the truth | Forgets about it and moves on | Hides the wrong idea from the pupils
test: Which word means a wrong idea that many people share? | Fact | Evidence | *Misconception | Question
test: In a text, which phrase tells you that many people wrongly believe something? | «a bright light» | «a long pause» | «a clear answer» | *«a common misconception»
exam: Which of these is a misconception? | Ice is cold | Multiplying by 2 makes a number bigger | The Earth goes round the Sun | *Multiplying by 0.5 always makes a number bigger
